[](images/0c/0cadce3122d5f1b1cb91927b6ab867404b826925c7a7900127b3824e7e286d2b.svg) but\n![](images/6e/6ee449b923787429cd51ad69f53e3420d1f50561840e54227a5cac671a7343cf.svg)\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n![](images/2b/2bfb4950b5dc2d95b9328d8d923b66cdc19ff1608394aff61fb1ae7811ec8d21.svg)\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) is a saddle point of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186124379 1074 "The point ![](images/7a/7ac5ab8e5444901eb7e978a0916e953bbe6da7ff8fb8a481537ad1f07d5bf48d.png) is a critical point of the function ![](images/87/87f22c24143e0fdad241f6b070b0e0ba1d89b2d5c32669989a7c75df700f5e75.png) if ![](images/2f/2fa6429606f96f4c4f43b91de76acab79152fd9989108836084cea77e0d9db09.png) In such cases, we can classify ![](images/12/1287cfe4aa57a8ec4c01f84934cb29b3d524f0524a48e39d7f6a30aed97e8ee2.png) as follows:\n\n- if ![](images/f2/f267e59d3059eac794b9f97c216e0dafd1b01c00ea5a0466d01cdab5fdeef865.png) is positive definite, then ![](images/81/81a814457abc7dc83baedc3d0ccd3be23e5734c3634a0bc8c8ce7fca53cc9406.png) is a local minimum\n\n- if ![](images/91/91ecc7d66921c91ed5baf1b1d2453f9cc4678feed21f325291b38f103f0266b6.png) is negative definite, then ![](images/74/74bbb71a684ae2b42d8a3779704a0c8b47ef43e5cebd265861418f77cd2b6267.png) is a local maximum\n\n- if ![](images/f2/f267e59d3059eac794b9f97c216e0dafd1b01c00ea5a0466d01cdab5fdeef865.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then ![](images/81/81a814457abc7dc83baedc3d0ccd3be23e5734c3634a0bc8c8ce7fca53cc9406.png) is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have ![](images/1a/1ada0c06e92bc2e76f33fade0b4f28728f641cc98923f855735c118d2ea0ba25.png) Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ![](images/29/29aa6115eeac65ed8ca2194e8fdce4de48591b144de9ee298e7c151f78bda4ac.png) for ![](images/ff/ffb48b96eb46bc28ce8546552edbc23496ae84720dc2cc4413cc7425b056bba0.png)  denote the leading principal minors of an ![](images/36/363026acf3cf01ffee6edd4166c849b1cce0cfd1e14af8dc3a15fa2a2f41163d.png) matrix ![](images/50/50946bb3afa72276eb132efe89bbe450df8836747fa6d11d04dbb142da7f6fad.png) Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ![](images/40/407d3bd58712c649860e47e39322f4471da8446ed312741900b2697fafe9d4a5.png) (as well as the matrix ![](images/84/849d81b747384fa1c4d5194327dd049207fdb7bf2db258e117c7214f870555b3.png)) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ![](images/a9/a93d47764597bd39da3f5bb625a6d1977c61f6a731e8b041850fef5dc9c7bc52.png) for all ![](images/81/8161c7c2b38d84c7033c688b3b41d0decc2861cd218f08dc3f87625eed2cc376.png)\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all ![](images/12/127d0aace5f471d897e0b6171e68da877234f9b709bf379245032522de9f6aa5.png) we have ![](images/b1/b18d3603f870bb527a4ee4618ed482165e62659a001cf8f2bbef4503c249b313.png) when ![](images/54/541f4f6d68d831274235815dceef666c2a59172ab8825d63aa189957530d9ce9.png) is odd, and ![](images/32/32a94e6ea1436d78a428c8ff22e50a7df311609ab74824c672abd2334c83988a.png) when ![](images/ae/aebdc0a3ecd72c42a452c68c6019eff7ad41de49b8a09517f7fd2ff8df58e32f.png) is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ![](images/fc/fcb57eaada70434abbf4a6056c9a8a793f6a0348196813e6a512de4772290f8e.png) but\n![](images/ae/ae6614c135f2e9d1d011de5f58d17fb4e43c2c6b87a7831a57b1287cf1a6e13d.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n![](images/18/1851f51854993b0360e75b0fc717263a7a84938b86ae515d74be32a0ac87362d.png)\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix ![](images/79/7906e975aaeffa8eae6d319b4be2d6a585c41b95ec59d721eedb7355fda29066.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix ![](images/33/3397629141c3034fbd4b5cd5bd8c554676175a4b1e3f5eccd26cc5d66ee1bec8.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point ![](images/27/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) is a saddle point of ![](images/4f/4f118f558380017fb67aa8f0b030dfa06a10216b7381582c619907c32988f211.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534703 17592186045417 false] [17592186124646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433377 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186124646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433378 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186124646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433379 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186124646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433380 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186124736 1050 660.0 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186125816 1064 "So, given a stationary critical point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R},$ how do we classify it?\n\nOne way is to consider the Taylor expansion of $f$ about $\\mathbf{a}{:}$\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ is a stationary point, we have $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ This gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nLet's assume that $\\mathbf x$ is close to $\\mathbf a,$ $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0,$ and that the terms of cubic order and higher are sufficiently small. Then, we have\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is an $n \\times n$ *symmetric* matrix. Thus, the term\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nis a *quadratic form*.\n\nTherefore, we note the following:\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, this quadratic form is always positive, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) > 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) > f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *minimum*.\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, this quadratic form is always negative, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) < 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) < f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *maximum.*\n\n- If this quadratic form is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point.\n\nThe assumption $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0$ is important. If $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) =0,$ then the quadratic form is zero along some line through $\\mathbf a$ or zero *everywhere* in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf a.$ In such cases, we cannot ignore the higher-order terms and must use another method to classify the critical point.\n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider cases where $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0.$" 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186125816 1064 "So, given a stationary critical point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R},$ how do we classify it?\n\nOne way is to consider the Taylor expansion of $f$ about $\\mathbf{a}{:}$\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ is a stationary point, we have $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ This gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nLet's assume that $\\mathbf x$ is close to $\\mathbf a,$ $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0,$ and that the terms of cubic order and higher are sufficiently small. Then, we have\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is an $n \\times n$ *symmetric* matrix. Thus, the term\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nis a *quadratic form*.\n\n\\[ \n\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf a)\n=\n\\left.\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_1^2} & \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1\\partial x_2} & \\cdots & \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_1 \\partial x_n} \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_2\\partial x_1 } & \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x_2^2} & \\cdots & \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_2 \\partial x_n} \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots \\\\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_n\\partial x_1 } & \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x_n\\partial x_2} & \\cdots & \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x_n^2}\n\n\\end{bmatrix}\n\\right|_{\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, we note the following:\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, this quadratic form is always positive, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) > 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) > f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *minimum*.\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, this quadratic form is always negative, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) < 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) < f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *maximum.*\n\n- If this quadratic form is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point.\n\nThe assumption $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0$ is important. If $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) =0,$ then the quadratic form is zero along some line through $\\mathbf a$ or zero *everywhere* in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf a.$ In such cases, we cannot ignore the higher-order terms and must use another method to classify the critical point.\n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider cases where $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0.$\n\nFor example, suppose we're told that [math]\\mathbf{a}[/math] is a critical point of the function [math]f[/math] and\n\n\\[\n\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}) =\n\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThen, we can classify the critical point by classifying [math]\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})[/math] as the matrix of a quadratic form.\n\nCalculating the eigenvalues of the matrix, we find that one is positive and the other is negative:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\lambda_1 &= -2 < 0 \\\\[3pt]\n\\lambda_2 &= 3 > 0\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, [math]\\mathbf{a}[/math] is a saddle point of [math]f.[/math]" 13194139534703 17592186045417 false] [17592186125817 1064 "Recall that the *derivative* of a multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by the matrix\n\n![](images/b5/b5dfe5fbad89ef3c6231dafe40d719be1faf007f782342abd6475756eda97b1e.svg)\n\nAnalogously with functions of one variable, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is a **critical point** of $f$ if $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ or does not exist.\n\n- Critical points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ are known as **stationary points.**\n\nThe local extrema of a function $f$ occur at the critical points. So, we can find the local extrema of $f$ by determining the stationary points *and* the points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ does not exist and classify them accordingly (we'll discuss how to do this shortly). \n\nNot all of the solutions of $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ give rise to local extrema! Solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ that *do not* give rise to local extreme values are called **saddle points**.\n\nFor example, consider the hyperbolic paraboloid \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = y^2-x^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/cea092da684db6b055c78369fcb987c3577a027ef0d8f03d598e115e85775ff5.png)\n\nIt's easy to check that $\\boldsymbol f'(0,0) = \\mathbf 0^T,$ and so $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f.$ This point is a saddle point. Note that the surface curves up in one direction (the red curve) and down in a different direction (the green curve), resembling a horse riding saddle.\n\nIn any neighborhood of a saddle point $\\mathbf a,$ there are points $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ such that $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'-\\mathbf{a}$ are orthogonal and they satisfy\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf a) >0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad f(\\mathbf{x}') - f(\\mathbf a) <0.\n$$" 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186125817 1064 "Recall that the *derivative* of a multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAnalogously with functions of one variable, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is a **critical point** of $f$ if $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ or does not exist.\n\n- Critical points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ are known as **stationary points.**\n\nThe local extrema of a function $f$ occur at the critical points. So, we can find the local extrema of $f$ by determining the stationary points *and* the points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ does not exist and classify them accordingly (we'll discuss how to do this shortly). \n\nNot all of the solutions of $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ give rise to local extrema! Solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ that *do not* give rise to local extreme values are called **saddle points**.\n\nFor example, consider the hyperbolic paraboloid \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = y^2-x^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/cea092da684db6b055c78369fcb987c3577a027ef0d8f03d598e115e85775ff5.png)\n\nIt's easy to check that $\\boldsymbol f'(0,0) = \\mathbf 0^T,$ and so $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f.$ This point is a saddle point. Note that the surface 