ny global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=1.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$ and  $(x,y)=(1,0),$so the global maximum points are $(-1,-2,1)$ and $(1,0,1).$\n\n![](images/4a/4a818d3e3e3c6c6a90ff9e69d808803016114894fbc605de5d21c75817eb8cf3.png)" 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186433380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433374 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186433380 1069 "q-163792" 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186433380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186433380 1071 "![](images/37/3764e985ef3b5c6b8c1bb5a3d347c52ef8a2a87edef3118c70da98f92fbd5d00.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?" 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186433380 1072 #uuid "dbf39e0a-85ba-5f58-ae9c-696c73732745" 13194139534703 17592186045417 true] [17592186433380 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-3.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-3.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,-1),$ and  $(x,y)=(-1,2),$ so the global minimum points are $(1,-1,-3)$ and $(-1,2,-3).$\n\n![](images/7d/7d62c7d1b3a265608fd93f395612092e1643858b1515f0e4d7a313b82b4c8f78.png)" 13194139534703 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-085288bf-eb67-57c6-970e-1441a16de2cc" 17592186433353 "answer-0c750366-87fd-5df1-9550-7377df9d1451" 17592186433354 "answer-2390596a-df7b-5124-a17c-f037210598e2" 17592186433355 "answer-37d1cb39-5327-5d1b-968d-afe88f5dcd13" 17592186433356 "answer-48052d29-4422-5f9a-b0b0-95c416d880f9" 17592